{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1444211-w20-questions-regarding-two-silent-strider-gifts","id":1444211,"title":"W20: Questions regarding two Silent Strider Gifts","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-04-16T08:48:24-07:00","id":1444211,"title":"W20: Questions regarding two Silent Strider Gifts","content":"Hello everybody,<br /> <br /> in the past I got great Advice regarding the Silent Striders and their Relashionship with Ghosts and Spirits on this forum.<br /> <br /> Today I come with another questions which I imagine goes in a similiar direction.<br /> <br /> So I read up on the two Silent Strider Gifts &quot;Mark of the Death-Wolf&quot; and &quot;Black Mark&quot;.<br /> <br /> The former leaves an area &quot;badly haunted by ghosts if they are in the area&quot; the later does the same but it can be inflicted upon an enemy for example.<br /> <br /> While none of our Pack is even remotely Rank 3 or 4 I find these two gifts very interesting, but the description of the consequenzes are left vague.<br /> <br /> Consindering they are Gifts of Rank 3 and 4 they must be quite severe I Imagine.<br /> <br /> Could you give me some insight on what could happen in a &quot;haunted area&quot; or to a &quot;haunted person&quot;? How would you, as an ST, handle the gifts?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25379-spacedigger","name":"Spacedigger","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-17T00:27:31-07:00","id":1444311,"title":"","content":"If you were using Wraith, it would take some work to translate that into appropriate mechanics. And it is probably not worth your time and effort spent to do so.<br /> <br /> To emulate this, I would use the Haunted flaw as the example I'd follow. Unfortunately, it doesn't appear to be in the W20 rules so you'll need to get it elsewhere. Below is an example of what I found online, but I don't know what edition of Vampire it is from. And you can find other variations of this Flaw online. Make adjustments as needed. In terms of the gift, the reason the spirit is haunting the victim is not because of anything personal, but because somehow the &quot;mark&quot; left on him attracts or compels that kind of attention.<br /> <br /> <i>Haunted (3pt. Flaw)<br /> You are haunted by an angry and tormented spirit, most likely one of your first victims. This spirit actively attempts to hinder you, especially when feeding, and does its utmost to vent its anguish upon you and anyone in your presence. The Storyteller determines the exact nature of the spirit, its powers, and whether or not it can eventually be laid to rest.</i><br /> <br /> It's a three point flaw, so you may also want to use similar three point flaws, like Cursed, as an example to follow.<br /> <br /> In terms of what the ghosts do, think of ghost stories you actually know. Movies like The Amityville Horror, Exorcist, Poltergeist, and Hell House are all extreme examples of what a really bad haunting could be. Most fictional ghost stories are not that bad, and real life reports of hauntings are even more tame (though still frightening to people who experience them). So you need to decide what is appropriate for you. The effects of the Gift probably are not consistent because it relies on factors the Silent Strider cannot control.<br /> <br /> If you are looking for ghost stories you can read, I recommend the stories of M.R. James, the Carnacki Ghost Finder stories of William Hope Hodgson, and the stories of Oliver Onions. You can find nice Penguin editions of M.R. James now online or at your local bookstore. M.R. James is probably considered to be the greatest writer of ghost stories ever.<br /> <br /> And it's entirely possible that there aren't any ghosts in the area who could be immediately attracted to whatever the Silent Strider does. So you may want to determine if there are any ghosts in the area based on the area's population, history, and reputation for ghosts. It's just another complication.<br /> <br /> Keep in mind that not every &quot;ghost&quot; is a wraith. It could be a wraith in catharsis to its shadow. It could be spectres. It could even be some strange plasmic creature. So you have a lot of leeway to explain things in case anyone objects and refers to Wraith.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-17T04:33:14-07:00","id":1444323,"title":"","content":"Thank your for your input <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> The haunted flaw is actually a great example. Thankfully I own all V20 Books so I can look it up with no problem.<br /> <br /> As within my last thread about Ghosts and Silent Striders naturally one arrives at WTO. I suggested to my Storyteller to have a look but I can not burden him with reading through another Corebook just so my Charakter hast a better Ark. Considering the Haunted Flaw puts the possibilitys entirely in the Sts Hands that might be the way to go.<br /> <br /> The authors you mention might be a good read regardless. Big fan of a good spook story^^<br /> <br /> Essentially I wanted to know if the Ghosts in question could actually hurt someone or something with their haunting as your average wrathful Poltergeist can do. But since you mentioned The Exorcist and Poltergeist that seems to be a given.<br /> <br /> I really love the Silent Strider Gift Selection because of utility and theme, but sadly many of them are not straightforward as other tribe gifts are and rarely seem to be relevant. At least in the chronicle I play.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25379-spacedigger","name":"Spacedigger","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-17T14:02:20-07:00","id":1444386,"title":"","content":"It's rare for a wraith to be able to hurt someone across the Shroud. It costs a lot of XP to build up the proper stats and certain Arcanoi to their highest level. But the Arcanoi are intended to simulate ghost stories, and some Arcanoi are intended to be used across the Shroud.<ul><li><b>Outrage</b> emulates most poltergeist activity. Things get thrown around.</li> <li><b>Pandemonium</b> emulates a lot of classic haunting effects found in movies - people see blood running down walls, hear strange, frightening noises, and other illusions that affect your senses but then disappear so there's no proof.</li> <li><b>Flux</b> emulates a variety of effects that can be said to represent the growth of entropy of the world. Mold, lichen, etc, grow rapidly on walls and floors. Paper yellows with age, wood rots, fabrics become fragile, etc. Then finally the laws of reality breakdown to allow the wraith to do things like animate puppets, stuffed animals, marbles on the floor, hat racks, etc. as well as build a &quot;body&quot; from discarded materials - the typical figure that arises from a pile of laundry, or garbage in an alley.</li> <li><b>Puppetry</b> allows a wraith to possess somebody, starting first with just experiencing what it's like to be in a body again (controlled by the real person) to slowly making inadvertent or temporary movements of limbs, to finally flat out control of the entire body. A Dark Arcanoi of the Spectres that is similar is called <b>Corruption</b>, except it works on the principle of first corrupting the soul to make one more like the Spectre until the Spectre can take over. If the first Arcanoi is like a ghost speaking through a medium, then the second Arcanoi is more like your demonic possession.</li> </ul>Those are the four (or five) big ones that produce effects across the Shroud, but so do some other powers. And of course Phantasm allows a wraith to enter a person's dreams (as do some of the Galliard gifts!).<br /> <br /> So if you can imagine it in a ghost story, a wraith can probably do it. It's just that your standard Wraith can't do everything, just like your standard Kindred lacks all the typical vampires of a vampire because they're divided between lots of Disciplines.<br /> <br /> Now most wraiths can't do these. The biggest effects take a lot of XP to get to. And these Arcanoi are either highly regulated or outlawed entirely by the Hierarchy. But they are out there.<br /> <br /> So an ST may want to emulate this in many ways, depending on how often the Gift is used and other factors. Having no haunting at all is just as valid as a slight haunting that has people scratching their heads, never realizing they've been haunted; to people knowing they are being haunted with the typical things one expects from haunting - some obvious, but infrequent and minor physical affects; to the truly terrifying and over-the-top haunting of certain movies that only happens in fiction.<br /> <br /> One thing that gets overlooked with wraith crossover with other games is that there is a belief that people need real supernatural power to cast any effective wards or protections against wraiths - vampires using the right Thumaturgy rituals, the right Theurge Gifts, Sorcery Paths, True Faith etc. But in the Wraith book itself, it makes it clear that ordinary people who truly believe in folkloric remedies can effectively, if only temporarily, create Wards and Forbiddances by using Willpower alone. So hauntings should never be a case where the victim is powerless against them. It may just take some effort to find someone who can help them which in the end might be an ordained minister/priest, a medium, or even the little old lady from the old country.<br /> <br /> Instead of fearing that these defenses can ruin your plot or effect, a good ST may just decide the haunting takes over forms while it avoids the warded area or person.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-17T22:46:06-07:00","id":1444451,"title":"","content":"Again thank your for your detailed elaboration.<br /> <br /> Going to pass this on to my ST. Makes the task quite less daunting for him I guess.<br /> <br /> Last Question:<br /> <br /> The Rank 1 Silent Strider Gift &quot;Visions of Duat&quot; allows the Strider to see ghosts in an area if their are present.<br /> <br /> So as I understand it the charakter could &quot;check&quot; an area and if ghosts are present they could use for example &quot;Mark of the Death-Wolf&quot; and a person that enters the &quot;marked&quot; area might be haunted by present ghosts? The Gift in question does state it leaves the area &quot;badly haunted&quot; but if the present &quot;ghosts&quot; have no ill will towards the victim or are not powerful enough would something happen to them still?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25379-spacedigger","name":"Spacedigger","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-18T00:22:52-07:00","id":1444455,"title":"","content":"I think that gets back to my earlier comment that it would take some work to translate the Gift into appropriate mechanics for Wraith. There's no existing mechanics that would explain why wraiths with no prior involvement would want to haunt someone. No crossover rules. So we'd need to come up with reasons that would make sense given the setting and mechanics of WtO.<br /> <br /> Some possibilities include: the Mark activates the Shadows of wraiths and assigns the victim some unconscious intent to the Shadow, and the wraiths fall in Catharsis; that the victim becomes the focus of some kind of Passion to all nearby wraiths, compelling them to haunt the victim in order to regain Pathos; the area might become a Haunt with a temporary reduction in the Shroud that would attract wraiths to the area; it might make the area a temporary Fetter for every wraith that gets near it; it might act as mass kind of compulsion based on the mechanics of Intimation; it might implant a Nihil that attracts Spectres from the Tempest; it could be a kind of radio beacon that transmits to the Hive-Mind of Spectres. It could be a combination of things. Or something else I haven't considered.<br /> <br /> It seems to me that the only way the Gifts can work is through some kind of supernatural compulsion. Otherwise the wraiths have no reason to haunt the victim or area. Depending on how the ST wants to interpret the Gift, there can be many ways it would be implemented.<br /> <br /> At the low end, if all the Mark does is provides some benefits of a Haunt, Fetter, or mild Passion, wraiths might interpret it as fairly benign or even beneficial. But it might lead to fairly passive and benign &quot;hauntings.&quot; At the other end, it might be considered vile necromancy that would anger the local wraithly population. But you'd get more obvious and spectacular hauntings. And also the possibility of wraithly reprisals against the &quot;necromancer&quot; who created the problem.<br /> <br /> = = =<br /> In any case, being able to see if there are local wraiths present would greatly benefit the decision of where and when to place such a Mark. As the Strider would know there's a greater chance of getting a pay off.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-18T02:44:54-07:00","id":1444469,"title":"","content":"Alright I think that clarifies things. Thank you.<br /> <br /> A classical Ghost Story approach might be the solution here, as you presented, without tapping into Wraith Territory to much. Similar to Giovanni/Cappadocian/Other Bloodline Necromancy from a VTM Perspective.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25379-spacedigger","name":"Spacedigger","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}